#{[17592186651984 :answer/value "8\\%"] [17592186651991 :answer/value "7.5\\%"] [17592186651995 :answer/value "9\\%"] [17592186651999 :answer/value "7.5\\%"] [17592186652053 :answer/value "6.5\\%"] [17592186652081 :answer/value "3\\%"] [17592186652097 :answer/value "4\\%"] [17592186652098 :answer/value "4.5\\%"] [17592186652103 :answer/value "6\\%"] [17592186652115 :answer/value "9\\%"] [17592186652118 :answer/value "7\\%"] [17592186652912 :question/worked-solution "We are told that the population is normally distributed, but we do not know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu$ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=80,\\,n=25,\\,s=6.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.797)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{24,0.005}=2.797.\n$$\n\n![](images/4f/4f9a0b7c9ec55a5c061f709b2d6fa02c3f760ccd66faab24c1201c4cf48bb18c.png)\n\nTherefore, a $99\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 80\\pm 2.797\\cdot \\frac{6}{\\sqrt{25}} \\\\  & 80\\pm 3.356\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186652959 :question/worked-solution "We are told that the population is normally distributed, but we do not know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu$ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=64,\\,n=16,\\,s=5.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.131)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{15,0.025}=2.131.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}64 & \\pm 2.131\\cdot \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{16}} \\\\ 64 & \\pm 2.664\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186661619 :answer/value "93.1\\%"] [17592186661644 :answer/value "93.5\\%"] [17592186661671 :answer/value "81.8\\%"] [17592186661691 :answer/value "89.3\\%"] [17592186661709 :answer/value "91.3\\%"] [17592186661721 :answer/value "83.9\\%"] [17592186661735 :answer/value "94.3\\%"] [17592186661777 :answer/value "93.4\\%"] [17592186661811 :answer/value "57.7\\%"] [17592186661863 :answer/value "80.8\\%"] [17592186661868 :answer/value "85.1\\%"] [17592186661876 :answer/value "93.3\\%"] [17592186661933 :answer/value "91.2\\%"] [17592186661942 :answer/value "64.4\\%"] [17592186661988 :answer/value "53.3\\%"] [17592186662029 :answer/value "87.1\\%"] [17592186662033 :answer/value "62.2\\%"] [17592186662071 :answer/value "94.5\\%"] [17592186662074 :answer/value "75.5\\%"] [17592186662081 :answer/value "88.7\\%"] [17592186662284 :question/worked-solution "We are told that the population is normally distributed. Therefore, the distribution of sample mean is also normal, and we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu$ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.05}=1.645.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}13\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\frac{7.5}{\\sqrt{36}} \\\\ 13\\pm 2.056\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186663373 :question/problem "What is the missing number?\n\n{{field-1}}\n$\\%$ of $36=27$"] [17592186663373 :question/worked-solution "We can write the given problem as the following product:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{}\\,\\%\\times 36=27\n$$\n\nUsing the relationship between multiplication and division, we can write our problem as:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{}\\,\\%=27÷36=\\frac{27}{36}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{27}{36}=\\frac{27÷9}{36÷9}=\\frac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nTo write this fraction as a percentage, we write an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{3\\times 25}{4\\times 25}=\\frac{75}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\frac{75}{100}=75\\,\\%\n$$"] [17592186663387 :question/problem "What is the missing number?\n\n$\\,$ $\\boxed{}\\,\\%$ of $5=9$"] [17592186663387 :question/worked-solution "We can write the given problem as the following product:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{}\\,\\%\\times 5=9\n$$\n\nUsing the relationship between multiplication and division, we can write our problem as:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{}\\,\\%=9÷5=\\frac{9}{5}\n$$\n\nTo write this fraction as a percentage, we write an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{9}{5}=\\frac{9\\times 20}{5\\times 20}=\\frac{180}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\frac{180}{100}=180\\%\n$$"] [17592186663389 :question/problem "What is the missing number?\n\n{{field-1}}\n$\\%$ of $80=24$"] [17592186663389 :question/worked-solution "We can write the given problem as the following product:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{}\\,\\%\\times 80=24\n$$\n\nUsing the relationship between multiplication and division, we can write our problem as:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{}\\,\\%=24÷80=\\frac{24}{80}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{24}{80}=\\frac{24÷8}{80÷8}=\\frac{3}{10}\n$$\n\nTo write this fraction as a percentage, we write an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{10}=\\frac{3\\times 10}{10\\times 10}=\\frac{30}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\frac{30}{100}=30\\,\\%\n$$"] [17592186663391 :question/problem "If $24\\times \\boxed{}\\,\\%=6,$ then $\\,\\boxed{}=$"] [17592186663391 :question/worked-solution "Using the relationship between multiplication and division, we can write our problem as:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{}\\,\\%=6÷24=\\frac{6}{24}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{6}{24}=\\frac{6÷6}{24÷6}=\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nTo write this fraction as a percentage, we write an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{1\\times 25}{4\\times 25}=\\frac{25}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\frac{25}{100}=25\\%\n$$"] [17592186663393 :question/problem "What is the missing number?\n\n$\\,$ $\\boxed{}\\,\\%$ of $10=3$"] [17592186663393 :question/worked-solution "We can write the given problem as the following product:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{}\\,\\%\\times 10=3\n$$\n\nUsing the relationship between multiplication and division, we can write our problem as:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{}\\,\\%=3÷10=\\frac{3}{10}\n$$\n\nTo write this fraction as a percentage, we write an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{10}=\\frac{3\\times 10}{10\\times 10}=\\frac{30}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\frac{30}{100}=30\\%\n$$"] [17592186663396 :question/problem "What is the missing number?\n\n{{field-1}}\n$\\%$ of $20=70$"] [17592186663396 :question/worked-solution "We can write the given problem as the following product:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{}\\,\\%\\times 20=70\n$$\n\nUsing the relationship between multiplication and division, we can write our problem as:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{}\\,\\%=70÷20=\\frac{70}{20}\n$$\n\nTo write this fraction as a percentage, we write an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{70}{20}=\\frac{70\\times 5}{20\\times 5}=\\frac{350}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\frac{350}{100}=350\\,\\%\n$$"] [17592186663401 :question/problem "What is the missing number?\n\n{{field-1}}\n$\\%$ of $24=12$"] [17592186663401 :question/worked-solution "We can write the given problem as the following product:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{}\\,\\%\\times 24=12\n$$\n\nUsing the relationship between multiplication and division, we can write our problem as:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{}\\,\\%=12÷24=\\frac{12}{24}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{12}{24}=\\frac{12÷12}{24÷12}=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTo write this fraction as a percentage, we write an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1\\times 50}{2\\times 50}=\\frac{5